 # Voxel Cone Tracing
 
-Also called: VCTGI, Voxel Cone Tracing for Global Illumination, Voxel-based cone tracing
+Also called: VCTGI, VXGI (Voxel Global Illumination), VXGINode, Voxel Cone Tracing for Diffuse GI, Voxel Cone Tracing for Global Illumination, Voxel-based cone tracing
 
 ## Overview
-Voxel Cone Tracing (VCT) is a real-time global illumination technique that approximates indirect lighting by voxelizing the scene into a 3D texture or sparse voxel octree and sampling it via expanding cones [S2][S3, S24, unofficial]. It solves the problem of computing multiple light bounces interactively by collapsing the hemispherical integral of the rendering equation into a sum of cone traces, allowing diffuse and specular reflections to be estimated with significantly lower cost than full path tracing [S2][S3, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from [Spherical Harmonic Volumetric Visibility](1225-spherical-harmonic-volumetric-visibility.md) by supporting glossy/specular reflections natively without basis function approximation, and from [Froxel-based Volumetric Rendering](1919-froxel-based-volumetric-rendering.md) by using a global hierarchical structure (octree or dense 3D texture) rather than camera-centric frustums [S3, S25, unofficial].
+Voxel Cone Tracing (VCT) is a real-time global illumination technique that approximates indirect lighting by voxelizing the scene into a 3D texture or sparse voxel octree and sampling it via expanding cones [S2][S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem of computing multiple light bounces interactively by collapsing the hemispherical integral of the rendering equation into a sum of cone traces, allowing diffuse and specular reflections to be estimated with significantly lower cost than full path tracing [S2][S3, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from [Spherical Harmonic Volumetric Visibility](1225-spherical-harmonic-volumetric-visibility.md) by supporting glossy/specular reflections natively without basis function approximation, and from [Froxel-based Volumetric Rendering](1919-froxel-based-volumetric-rendering.md) by using a global hierarchical structure (octree or dense 3D texture) rather than camera-centric frustums [S3, S25, unofficial].
 
